 1 big thing: TV networks band together 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photos: Getty Images The five major TV networks, which share responsibility for pooled video coverage of President Trump, have ceased that work in solidarity with the three outlets he banned from the White House, Axios' Sara Fischer reports. 

 ABC News, CBS News, NBC News, Fox News and CNN said in an unusual joint statement: "The public has a vital interest in receiving accurate, independent information about its government. No administration should restrict a news organization because it objects to its reporting." 📺 By backing CNN, MS NOW and Politico, the TV giants signaled that they're willing to use their combined leverage to pressure the White House into reinstating access.

 The networks' power is practical and cultural: They collectively manage the pooled video that puts the presidency on television, the medium that made Trump a national celebrity and that he has long closely monitored. 🧑‍⚖️ CNN, MS NOW, and Politico today filed a joint First Amendment lawsuit , arguing that the president and his administration violated their rights by blocking access over the weekend.

 The lawsuit is a historic show of solidarity amongst the banned outlets. Go deeper . (Disclosure: Sara is a CNN contributor.) 

 
 
