 2. ⛈️ El Niño triggers California statewide emergency 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Homes damaged by flooding on Sept. 8 in Dana Point, Calif., south of Los Angeles. Photo: Kayla Bartkowski/Los Angeles Times via Getty Images California Gov. Gavin Newsom (D) declared a state of emergency today amid a potentially historic El Niño weather event.

 Newsom's office says the move "directs state agencies to begin protecting vulnerable communities, prepare roads and critical infrastructure, stage emergency supplies, and help local partners reduce flood, landslide, and coastal risks." 🌊 El Niño — characterized by warmer-than-usual ocean surface temperatures in the Pacific — can drive more potent and more frequent storms and coastal flooding in California.

 Some coastal California towns have already been dealing with erosion and property damage tied to recent storm activity. Newsom's office noted the climate change link: "A study published in the journal Science last month found El Niño events are now more than 36% stronger than before the industrial era, with this trend accelerating over the last 40 years as the planet warms."

